      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "Endocannabinoid receptor 1 blockade is proposed to improve metabolic complications of obesity via central and peripheral effects."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Our objective was to test whether rimonabant improves insulin regulation of free fatty acid and glucose metabolism after controlling for fat loss."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "This was a double-blind, placebo-controlled substudy of the visceral fat reduction assessed by computed tomography scan on rimonabant (VICTORIA) trial."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS AND SETTING",
          "content": "Sixty-seven abdominally obese, metabolic syndrome volunteers age 35-70 yr participated at academic medical center general clinical research centers."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "Intervention included a 12-month lifestyle weight management program plus rimonabant 20 mg/d or placebo."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Body composition and two-step euglycemic, hyperinsulinemic clamp before and after intervention were performed. Insulin sensitivity was assessed as insulin concentration needed to suppress by 50% palmitate concentration [IC50(palmitate)], flux [IC50(palmitate)f], and hepatic glucose output [IC50(HGO)] and as insulin-stimulated glucose disposal (Δ glucose disappearance per Δ insulin concentration--glucose slope)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Body fat decreased by 4.5±2.9% (SD) in the rimonabant and 1.9±4.5% in the placebo group (P<0.005). The primary [improvement in IC50(palmitate) and IC50(palmitate)f] and secondary [improvement in IC50(HGO) and glucose slope] outcomes were not significantly different between the rimonabant and placebo groups. Post hoc analyses revealed that 1) changes in body mass index (BMI) and IC50(palmitate) were correlated (P=0.005) in the rimonabant group; this relationship was not significantly different from placebo when controlling for greater BMI loss (P=0.5); 2) insulin-regulated glucose disposal improved in both groups (P=0.002) and correlated with changes in BMI."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Improvements observed in insulin regulation of free fatty acid and glucose metabolism with rimonabant treatment in humans was not greater than that predicted by weight loss alone."
        }
      ]},
